  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Widespread showers and widely scattered \nthunderstorms will develop today, with drying conditions \nreturning for the early half of the coming week.\n\nStarting the day with a stationary front draped east to west \nnear the Virginia/North Carolina state border. Muggy conditions \nare in place areawide, with occasional showers developing and \nfading again per radar imagery since 1 am. \n\nFor today, coverage of rainfall will remain isolated to widely \nscattered through mid-afternoon, with coverage then increasing \nas the front lifts northward across the lower Mid-Atlantic. \nBelieve that, while shower activity will become widespread \nduring the late afternoon and evening, only moderate available \nenergy will limit the coverage of thunderstorms to widely \nscattered activity, though the greater coverage will occur \nacross the Piedmont where temperatures will warm into the mid \n80s. Any thunderstorms will be capable of producing heavy \ndownpours that could result in localized flash flooding issues, \nand one or two storms may intensify briefly to also become \ncapable of producing damaging wind gusts. The front will shift \nnorthward out of the area shortly after sunset, with rainfall \nthen gradually tapering during early tonight.\n\nIsolated showers and thunderstorms may develop Sunday afternoon\nas another cold front approaches, but limited energy will keep \nthis activity weak. With the front passing across the lower\nMid-Atlantic on Sunday night, drier and cooler air will arrive \nfor the beginning of the coming workweek, perhaps lasting \nthrough Wednesday as a large upper ridge develops along the \nAppalachian Chain. By Thursday, afternoon showers and \nthunderstorms will gradually return as another cold front\napproaches from the northwest, which may stall across the \nregion during next weekend.",
